V100 (192.168.7.3) TurboQuant KV + correctness, 2026-09-26
repo: ~/llama-cpp-sm70 @ 793dc79a2
model: Qwen3.8-27B-Uncensored-HauhauCS-Aggressive-IQ4_XS.gguf (14.62 GiB)
env: GGML_V100_FA=0, GGML_CUDA_FORCE_MMQ unset, all runs -b 2048 -ub 2048

================================================================
1. correctness: does the -ub 2048 change alter the output?
================================================================
llama-server, temp 0, seed 1234, prompt "The capital of France is", n_predict 32:

--- ub=512
  text: ' Paris.\nThe capital of Germany is Berlin.\nThe capital of Italy is Rome.\nThe capital of Spain is Madrid.\nThe capital of Portugal is'
  prompt_n=5 pp=25.8 tg=37.37
--- ub=2048
  text: ' Paris.\nThe capital of Germany is Berlin.\nThe capital of Italy is Rome.\nThe capital of Spain is Madrid.\nThe capital of Portugal is'
  prompt_n=5 pp=25.6 tg=37.37

  Identical text, identical decode rate. The ubatch change is numerically safe
  for this workload. (It changes the GEMM M dimension, so this is evidence, not
  a proof; re-check on any model whose quantized path is accumulation-order
  sensitive.)

================================================================
2. TurboQuant KV on V100: capacity, not speed
================================================================
llama-bench -p <ctx> -n 128, ub=2048. "-ctk X -ctv X" is what was requested;
the K column shows what the fork's TURBO_AUTO_ASYMMETRIC=1 auto-selection
actually used (q8_0 K + turbo3 V for this GQA=6 model).

--- 32K context, r=2 ---
kv=f16/f16      pp32768  707.77 +- 9.94    tg128  27.16 +- 2.24
kv=q8_0/q8_0    pp32768  699.92 +- 3.04    tg128  27.02 +- 2.12
kv=q8_0/turbo3  pp32768  707.08 +- 7.51    tg128  26.31 +- 1.93
kv=q8_0/turbo4  pp32768  701.80 +- 6.29    tg128  26.79 +- 2.07

--- 64K context, r=1 ---
kv=f16/f16      pp65536  596.09            tg128  28.16
kv=q8_0/turbo3  pp65536  564.50            tg128  26.45

  Verdict: no speed benefit at 32K (all within 1 sigma), and a 5.3% prefill /
  6.1% decode LOSS at 64K. TurboQuant KV is a memory-capacity feature on this
  hardware, not a speed feature.

  Why: decode at 32K is 27 tok/s = 37 ms/token while the working set is
  14.6 GB weights + 2 GB KV = 16.6 GB, i.e. 451 GB/s effective against a
  ~900 GB/s peak. Decode is latency/occupancy bound, not byte bound, so
  removing KV bytes buys nothing, while the turbo path adds dequant work
  inside the attention kernel.

  KV size per token for this model (16 full-attn layers of 64, 4 KV heads,
  D=256): 4 x 256 x 2 x 2 B x 16 = 64 KiB at f16, ~13 KiB at turbo3.
  So KV is 12% of the decode working set at 32K and 23% at 64K.

  This is the fork's headline feature, and it does not pay off on V100.
